# Controllable Heterogenous Seeding‐Induced Crystallization for High‐Efficiency FAPbI3‐Based Perovskite Solar Cells Over 24%

## abstract
The addition of small seeding particles into a supersaturated solution is one among the most effective approaches to obtain high-quality semiconductor materials via increased crystallization rates. However, limited study is conducted on this approach for the fabrication of perovskite solar cells. Here, a new strategy-heterogenous seeding-induced crystallization (hetero-SiC) to assist the growth of FAPbI3 -based perovskite is proposed. In this work, di-tert-butyl(methyl)phosphonium tetrafluoroborate is directly introduced into the precursor, which forms a low-solubility complex with PbI2 . The low-solubility complex can serve as the seed to induce crystallization of the perovskite during the solvent-evaporation process. Various in situ measurement tools are used to visualize the hetero-SiC process, which is shown to be an effective way of manipulating the nucleation and crystal growth of perovskites. The hetero-SiC process greatly improves the film quality, reduces film defects, and suppresses nonradiative recombination. A hetero-SIC proof-of-concept device exhibits outstanding performance with 24.0% power conversion efficiency (PCE), well over the control device with 22.2% PCE. Additionally, hetero-SiC perovskite solar cell (PSC) stability under light illumination is enhanced and the PSC retains 84% of its initial performance after 1400 h of light illumination.

The addition of small seeding particles into a supersaturated solution is one among the most effective approaches to obtain high-quality semiconductor materials via increased crystallization rates. However, limited study is conducted on this approach for the fabrication of perovskite solar cells. Here, a new strategy—“heterogenous seeding-induced crystallization (hetero-SiC)” to assist the growth of $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ -based perovskite is proposed. In this work, di-tert-butyl(methyl)phosphonium tetrafluoroborate is directly introduced into the precursor, which forms a low-solubility complex with $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ . The low-solubility complex can serve as the seed to induce crystallization of the perovskite during the solvent-evaporation process. Various in situ measurement tools are used to visualize the hetero-SiC process, which is shown to be an effective way of manipulating the nucleation and crystal growth of perovskites. The hetero-SiC process greatly improves the film quality, reduces film defects, and suppresses nonradiative recombination. A hetero-SIC proof-of-concept device exhibits outstanding performance with $24.0\%$ power conversion efficiency (PCE), well over the control device with $22.2\%$ PCE. Additionally, hetero-SiC perovskite solar cell (PSC) stability under light illumination is enhanced and the PSC retains $84\%$ of its initial performance after $\mathtt{l400h}$ of light illumination.
## 1. Introduction
Organic–inorganic metal halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have attracted great interest over the last decades and obtained a certified power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $25.7\%$ .[1] The solutionprocessed fabrication of PSCs provides a huge advantage over other techniques to obtain cost-effective solar cells with a facile manufacturing process.[2] However, the slow crystallization process of solution-processed perovskite will lead to a nonuniform and nonfully covered film with large crystals.[2–4] Several strategies were introduced to manipulate the perovskite crystallization, including antisolvent quenching,[3,5] gas quenching,[6] vacuum quenching,[7] elevated temperature coating, and so on. The majority of the aforementioned strategies focus on facilitating solvent evaporation to promote rapid saturation and faster crystallization.
Adding small crystal seeding is a classical approach in the semiconductor area to obtain high-quality semiconductor material (Si and GaAs) with reduced defect density under a high growth speed.[8,9] Inspired by this, the seed-induced crystallization is also adopted in PSCs fabrication.[10–18] The seed-induced crystallization is believed to be an effective approach to modulate the film formation process by reducing the critical Gibbs free energy of nucleation.[8] Based on the variety of seed material and target material, the seeding-induced crystallization (SiC) can be divided into heterogenous and homogenous (heteroSiC and homo-SiC). Enhancing the film quality and lowering defect density can be accomplished via homogenous-seedinginduced crystallization (homo-SiC) of perovskite.[10–16] Typically, the seeds applied in homo-SIC exhibit a lattice-matching with target perovskite to a certain extend. In the field of PSCs, the homo-SiC displays unique potential, particularly for the twostep fabrication of perovskite. Sargent’s group reported the perovskite seed crystal $(\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3})$ ) embedding in $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ film which acts as the nuclei to facilitate the crystallization during the two-step fabrication of perovskite.[11] With the perovskite seed crystal, the PSCs show a striking improvement in both stability and performance. On the basis of this method, Zhao’s group achieved better device performance by replacing CsI with CsCl to serve as the seed in the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ precursor ink.[13] They found that the $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ could retard the crystallization process and reduce the nucleation center, leading to an enlarged grain size and reduced defects. Besides these works, multiple seed materials were developed and added in the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ precursor ink to serve as the seed in the two-step deposition method and achieve great performance enhancement, including mixed perovskite seed of Cs and Rb cations,[14,15] Br-rich perovskite seed,[16] and tertbutylpyridine (TBP).[17] Apart from introducing seed in $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ink of the two-step method, the seed assistant growth approach was also applied in one-step deposition method. Chen’s group developed one intermixing-seeded growth approach with the addition of MAI-capped PbS nanoparticles in the perovskite precursor solution.[18] The MAI-capped PbS can be functioned as effective heterogenous nucleation sites to promote the formation of the inorganic framework of perovskite lattice structures, leading to enhanced film coverage and improved performance. On the basis of this work, Chen’s group further revealed the “catalytic” role of $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{S}$ seed nanoparticle that the PbS NCs can catalyze the conversion of the intermediate phase into perovskite crystals by reducing the activation energy.[19] Meanwhile, alkali additives $(\mathrm{KPb}_{2}\mathrm{Br}_{5})$ were added in the precursor ink as crystallization seed to enable highly crystalline large-area perovskite film via slot-die coating.[20] CdSe colloidal quantum dots were also reported to serve as crystallization seeds to modulate the nucleation and crystal growth processes.[21]
Rather than directly adding the seed to the precursor, the formation of seed template at the bottom interface was also reported as an effective approach for the crystallization modulation. Gratzel’s group reported a simple approach by using a predeposited poly(methyl methacrylate) (PMMA) template as the seeds to induce the growth of high-quality perovskite film.[22] The PMMA template was found to enable heterogenous nucleation (hetero-SiC) of perovskite film with few defects and larger oriented grains. Han’ group reported a perovskite crystal array (PCA) method to assist the growth of the perovskite absorption layer.[23] The patterned PCA is regularly distributed on the substrate and served as templated crystal, which induced a wellorganized upward crystallization process.
The study on the seed assistant perovskite growth indeed acquires good progress. However, majority of the works were focused on the “two-step deposition” method and the studies on the “one-step deposition” method is limited. Therefore, we begin to develop a facile strategy to manipulate the crystallization process by directly introducing the seed material into the precursor solution in the “one-step” method of perovskite fabrication. The key challenge of it lies in the selection of seeding material, where an optimized material solubility should be selected. It is challenging to produce a smooth and uniform film if the seed material solubility is too low since the seeds would precipitate in the precursor and severely reduce film quality. On the other side, a high solubility material cannot act as the crystallization seed for the perovskite.
The organic ionic compounds with chemically stable organic cation were widely used as an additive for the perovskite. Several groups have revealed the ionic liquid/salts $(\mathrm{[BMM]BF_{4}},$ $\mathrm{[BMP]BF_{4},}$ et  al.) could passivate the defects and work as stabilizers for PSCs.[24–26] The incorporation of the ionic compound in the perovskite absorber could suppress deep trap states, boost $\mathrm{PSCs^{\prime}}$ performance and operation stability.[24,25,27,28] The organic ionic compounds are also used as interface modifiers for PSCs, such as hole or electron-transporting layer/ perovskite absorber interface.[25,26,29–32] These benefits led us to begin make a choice from the group of organic ionic compounds, and we discovered that di-tert-butyl(methyl)phosphonium tetrafluoroborate $((\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4})$ can form a low solubility complex with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in the majority of common solvents for perovskite precursors, including N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO), and 2-methoxyethanol. Furthermore, we systematically analyzed the whole nucleation and growth processes of perovskite with $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ by in situ characterization techniques (in situ absorption, X-ray diffraction (XRD), and so on), providing a new understanding of the seeded perovskite growth. Finally, the perovskite films produced by the hetero-SiC method have superior quality, low trap density, and a larger grain size. The hetero-SiC PSCs demonstrate outstanding performance with a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $24.0\%$ (well over the control device with $22.2\%$ . Furthermore, the hetero-SiC PSCs display significantly improved operation stability, maintaining $84\%$ of original performance after $1400\mathrm{h}$ of light illumination.
## 2. Results and Discussion
The $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ is a low-cost and widely used electronrich ligand (ionic salts) for palladium-catalyzed cross-coupling reactions. The chemical structure of $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ is shown in Figure  1B inset. In this work, we demonstrate a heterogenous-seeding-induced crystallization (hetero-SiC) approach to manipulate the crystallization process of perovskite via this low-cost ionic salts $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ . In this approach, a small amount of $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ is added into the precursor of $\mathrm{(FAPbI_{3})_{0.93}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.04}(C s P b I_{3})_{0.03}}$ perovskite—FAMACs perovskite and the additive can react with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ to form a low solubility complex. In order to help perovskite crystals grow, the low solubility complex will precipitate as nuclei seeds. The crystallization process of perovskite via hetero-SiC can be explained by the classical model of Gibbs free energy.
In classical theory, the crystallization of perovskite involved two important processes: nucleation and growth.[2] The film quality is highly related to the rates of these two processes. At first, the nucleation of fine particles is initiated from the supersaturated state.[2] For conventional homogenous nucleation, the nuclei formation can be considered as a thermodynamic equilibrium where the total free energy $(\Delta G)$ of a spherical particle is equal to the sum of the surface free energy $(\Delta G_{\mathrm{s}})$ and the bulk free energy $(\Delta G_{\mathrm{v}})$ . In a supersaturated solution, the $\Delta G_{\mathrm{s}}$ is a positive quantity proportional to $r^{2}$ and $\Delta G_{\mathrm{v}}$ is a negative quantity proportional to $r^{3}$ . The total free energy is defined as $\Delta G=\Delta G_{\mathrm{{S}}}+\Delta G_{\mathrm{{V}}}=4\pi r^{2}\gamma+{\frac{4}{3}}\pi r^{3}\Delta G_{\upsilon}$ , where $\gamma$ is the surface energy between the supersaturated solution and crystalline surface and $\Delta G_{\upsilon}$ is the bulk free energy per unit volume.
The seeding in the precursor is characterized by the dynamic light scattering measurement. From the scattering spectra, we find the colloids exhibit two dominant distributions (Figure 1A). With the addition of $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ , the colloids with the size between 100 and $300\ \mathrm{nm}$ (control precursor) are gradually grown to $400{-}2500~\mathrm{nm}$ (precursor with $1\mathrm{mol\%}$ additive) which indicates the growth of particle under different concentration of additive (peaks position shown in Figure  1A). These large colloids from the control precursor are mainly coming from perovskite natural clustering. With $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4},$ the size of colloids gradually increases which is due to the generation of a low solubility complex. In comparison with control one, the precursor with 0.3 and $0.5\mathrm{\mol}\%$ $\mathrm{(t{-}B u)_{2}P M e H B F_{4}}$ also exhibits a lower spectral intensity (particles size over $100\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) indicating a reduced nucleation center. This could be due to the small perovskite clusters being aggregated into large-size nucleation centers with the assistance of the low-solubility complex. From the optical image, we could also find amount of low-solubility white needle shape precipitated in the precursor with $1\mathrm{mol}\%$ $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ (Figure  1B inset photo). The XRD measurement was conducted on the films with and without $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ . Compared with the control film, a new peak around $8.8^{\mathrm{{o}}}$ is found in the XRD pattern of the film with $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ (Figure  1B) which is attributed to the newly formed white crystal. To reveal the origin of the newly generated phase, the $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ is added into the precursor solutions of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and FAI (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The white needle shape crystals only precipitate from the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ precursor solution with $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ . The XRD analysis also represents the same diffraction peak at $8.8^{\circ}$ from the $\mathrm{(t{\cdot}B u)_{2}P M e H B F_{4}{\mathrm{-}}P b I_{2}}$ film (Figure S2, Supporting Information). This indicates that the newly generated phase is “one complex” of $\mathrm{(t{\cdot}B u)_{2}P M e H B F_{4}{\mathrm{-}}P b I_{2}}$ . Furthermore, solutions with different ratios of $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ are also prepared. From the XRD pattern, we find a relatively pure phase with a negligible amount of residual $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ with the ratio of 1:2 $(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{:(t{\cdot}B u)}_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4})$ (Figure S2, Supporting Information). To obtain a smooth and uniform film, it is desirable to avoid the precipitation of complex in perovskite precursors. In order to demonstrate the following nucleation process, we first conducted a survey to establish which concentration produced the highest film quality and device performance and chose 0.3 $1\mathrm{mol\%}$ as the benchmark concentration.
To demonstrate the nucleation process, the perovskite precursor inks with and without $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ $(0.3\%$ mol) are prepared and dropped onto indium tin oxide (ITO) substrates followed by $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ thermal annealing. The whole nucleation and crystallization process is recorded by an optical microscope camera in real-time. The video frames at 0, 20, 25, and 30 s are grabbed and shown in Figure S3 in the Supporting Information. The scale bar in the frames is $300~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Initiated from $0\mathrm{~s~}$ , both two precursor droplets are clear. After $20~\mathrm{s}$ , a large amount of small black perovskite crystals appears in the precursor with $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ , while only a few crystals are formed in the control one. With the time going on (25 and 30  s), this difference between the two precursors becomes prominent and the perovskite crystals with $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ grow much larger, which indicated a faster crystallization process by hetero-SiC. This is because hetero-SiC could facilitate the nucleation process at nucleation sites (nuclei of $\mathrm{(t{\cdot}B u)_{2}P M e H B F_{4}{\mathrm{-}}P b I_{2}}$ complex), in contrast to homogenous nucleation (control precursor).
Besides the faster hetero-SiC nucleation process in the liquid phase, we also observe a faster crystallization process for the film prepared by a one-step spin-coating process (same as the device), characterized by in situ absorption measurement. The in situ absorption spectra show an absorption redshift at ${\approx}10$ s in both the control film and the film with $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ $(0.3\%$ mol) during the $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ annealing process (Figure  2A). The as-casted intermediate film exhibits only UV absorption at wavelengths shorter than $450~\mathrm{nm}$ which is attributed to the solvent complex. During the annealing process, the absorption at wavelengths longer than $450~\mathrm{nm}$ gradually increases, which is due to the phase transition from the intermediate phase and enhanced crystallinity of perovskite grain. The film with $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ demonstrates a faster crystallization process when compared to the control film, with the time of film absorption reaching the peak $10\mathrm{~s~}$ earlier. To quantify the difference between the two films, the in situ absorption color mapping is further interpreted by extracting the absorption intensity at the wavelength of $550~\mathrm{nm}$ and plotting the absorption intensity as a function of annealing time in Figure 2B. XRD measurements are also conducted to investigate the perovskite phase transition, shown in Figure  2C,D. The XRD patterns are collected from different annealing times (0, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 30, 60  s, 3, 5, and $10\ \mathrm{min}$ ). Before thermal annealing, two intermediate films (0 s) both exhibit three main peaks (denotes by $\&$ in the figure) at $6.6^{\circ}$ , $7.2^{\circ}$ , and $9.2^{\circ}$ which are corresponding to (002), (021), and (022) planes of MAI– $\mathrm{.PbI}_{2}$ –DMSO intermediate phase (Figure 2C,D).[5,36,37] Compared with hetero-SiC film, the control film exhibits much stronger diffraction peaks of nonperovskite phase $(\delta\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3})$ and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ (001) at $11.8^{\mathrm{o}}$ and $12.8^{\mathrm{o}}$ , respectively. This indicates that the addition of $\mathrm{(t{\cdot}B u)_{2}P M e H B F_{4}}$ could effectively suppress the formation of $\delta\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ in the intermediate stage. From the scanning electron microscope (SEM) images of the intermediate phase, the hetero-SiC film exhibits larger perovskite crystal grains with a clear grain boundary, while the control intermediate film shows lots of small grains without a clear grain boundary (Figure S4, Supporting Information). The white spots on the two films are the gold cluster which is from the gold deposition before SEM measurement to enhance the film conductivity for a better SEM quality. The different morphology of the two intermediate films could be attributed to the faster crystallization process by the hetero-SiC process.
After thermal annealing, the phase-conversion process from the intermediate phase to the perovskite phase is very fast.[36] The majority of the intermediate phase and ${\delta\mathrm{{FAPbI}}_{3}}$ phase are transformed into the perovskite phase at the first $3\mathrm{~s~}$ , according to the XRD measurement (Figure  2C,D). With the annealing process going on (3 and 5  s), a small peak at $8.8^{\mathrm{{o}}}$ is found in the hetero-SiC film which is corresponding to the complex of $\mathrm{(t{-}B u)_{2}P M e H B F_{4}{-}P b I_{2}}$ . While for the intermediate film (0  s), it is hard to distinguish the complex diffraction peak with (022) planes of the MAI–PbI2–DMSO intermediate phase as they are overlapped with each other. With a longer annealing process, the complex of $\mathrm{(t{\cdot}B u)_{2}P M e H B F_{4}{\mathrm{-}}P b I_{2}}$ is gradually reduced (after 5 s). This explains why we cannot find the peak of the complex from the XRD pattern of well-formed hetero-SiC perovskite film (Figure 2E). The majority of the remaining solvent evaporates during the first $10~\mathrm{s}$ , and the intermediate phase almost completely transforms into the perovskite phase. With the removal of residual solvent and conversion of intermediate phase (MAI– ${\mathrm{.PbI}}_{2}$ –DMSO), the absorption intensities of the two films gradually increase (Figure 2B). After $10~\mathrm{s}$ , the absorption intensities increase much faster which could be attributed to a faster crystal growth after solvent evaporation. Compared with the control film, the hetero-SiC film also exhibits a much faster crystallization process (Figure  2B). No significant difference is seen from the in situ XRD patterns (Figure  2C,D), indicating that the phase transition is not the main cause of the increasing absorption. From the SEM images, the dissolution and merging process of small particles into large crystals are observed (Figure S4, Supporting Information).[2] The fast perovskite crystal growth plays the dominant role in absorption changing. At last, the increase of the film absorption intensity becomes slower.
The complete perovskite films are obtained after $^{\textrm{1h}}$ thermal annealing. The grazing-incidence wide-angle X-ray scattering (GIWAXS) and XRD measurements are conducted onto the films with and without $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ (annealing for $60\mathrm{min}$ ) to investigate the crystallographic structure (Figure  2F). From the GIWAXS map, two films exhibit the same patterns with two diffraction rings located at $\boldsymbol{q}$ of 0.9 and $1\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}$ , which is corresponding to the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ phase and perovskite (100), respectively. From the XRD patterns (Figure 2E), we also find the same patterns of two films with the dominant peaks located at $12.65^{\circ}$ , $13.95^{\circ}$ , $24.45^{\circ}$ , and $28.2^{\circ}$ . These peaks are corresponding to the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , (100), (111), and (200) of $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite crystal planes, respectively. The hetero-SiC film with the well-controlled amount of $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ exhibits the same crystallographic structure and crystal orientation as the control film, demonstrating that the quicker seeding-induced crystallization does not alter the initial perovskite crystallographic structure (Figure  2E,F). Finally, we have also observed some intriguing phenomena. The GIWAXS map and XRD patterns for the film after $60\ \mathrm{min}$ of annealing reveal the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ peak, but the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ peak is barely detectable during the first $10\ \mathrm{min}$ of annealing (Figure 2C,D). In order to figure it, we then investigate the film annealing for 30 and $50\mathrm{min}$ . With the increasing of annealing time (from 30 to $50\ \mathrm{min}$ ), $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ peak intensity is gradually increasing (Figure S5, Supporting Information). This is conceivable because MACl evaporates during annealing over a longer period of time.
The time-of-flight secondary-ion mass spectrometry (ToFSIMS) is then conducted to investigate the chemical composition throughout the perovskite film (Figure  3B). The signal of $\mathrm{InO^{-}}$ indicates the touching of the substrate during the sputtering etching. From the result of ToF-SIMS, we find a well-distributed $\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeH^{+}}$ throughout the entire perovskite film, while the $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ is predominantly accumulated at the substrate which is consistent with the previous report that $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ is tended to accumulate at the metal oxide–perovskite interface.[25] The uniform distribution of (t-Bu $\mathrm{\Delta_{l}\mathrm{\pMeH^{+}}}$ also helps to facilitate the perovskite crystallization of the entire film. The crosssection and top-view field-emission SEM (FESEM) images of control film and hetero-SiC film are shown in Figure  3C,D. Compared with control film, the annealed hetero-SiC film exhibits a greatly enlarged grain size (largest one is around $2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}^{\mathrm{{\cdot}}}$ ), while the largest grain size of annealed control film is around $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . For a precise analysis, the grain size distribution is obtained from Figure 3D and Figure S5D in the Supporting Information. The average grain size of the control film is around $0.92\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . The perovskite grain size becomes larger with (t-Bu)2PMeHBF4, where the average grain size of films with $0.3\%$ and $0.5\%$ (t-Bu)2PMeHBF4 are 1.49 and $1.7\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ , respectively (Figure S6, Supporting Information). From the crosssection FESEM images, the film with $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ also exhibits a compact and smooth grain with enlarged grain size. The cross-section view of SEM image of the control film also shows a smaller grain size with a clear grain boundary which is consistent with the top-view FESEM image. The FESEM image demonstrates that the hetero-SiC approach could effectively facilitate the perovskite crystallization and lead to a high-quality perovskite film with enlarged grain size (Figure 3A).[11,22]
In addition, we also prepare types of related chemicals into the precursor to further study the proposed mechanism, including $\mathrm{KBF}_{4}.$ , $\mathrm{NaBF}_{4},$ and $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMe}$ . However, with the addition of the above-mentioned chemicals (from 0.1 to $5\%\mathrm{mol}$ ), no precipitate is found in the perovskite precursors. The FESEM measurement is then conducted onto the films with these additives. The morphologies of control film and films with $\mathrm{KBF}_{4}.$ , $\mathrm{NaBF_{4}}$ or $(\mathrm{t}{-}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMe}$ are very similar and no enlarged grain sizes are found (Figure S7, Supporting Information). However, the film with the $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ exhibits an obviously enlarged grain size. This could be due to the ${\mathrm{KBF}}_{4},$ ${\mathrm{NaBF}}_{4},$ and $(\mathrm{t}{-}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMe}$ cannot form low solubility seeds in the precursor, which further proves the function of seeding (heteroSiC process) on the film morphology.
To demonstrate the potential of the hetero-SiC approach on PSCs, the PSCs with the conventional structures are fabricated $\mathrm{\langleglass/ITO/SnO_{2}/}$ perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD/Au). The performances of PSCs with and without $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ are shown in Figure 4A and Table 1. The optimized performance of PSCs is obtained with $0.3\%$ mol (t-Bu) $)_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4},$ yielding a PCE of $24.0\%$ with a short circuit current $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ of $24.9\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , and open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ of $1.19~\mathrm{V},$ and an excellent fill factor (FF) of $81\%$ . In comparison, the control device exhibits a PCE of $22.2\%$ , $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $24.4~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.15~\mathrm{V},$ , and FF of $77\%$ . The hetero-SiC device exhibits great enhancement on both $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ $(40~\mathrm{mV}$ increment) and FF (from $77\%$ to $81\%$ . The statistical distribution of the photovoltaic parameters is obtained from 20 individual cells of each type (Figure S8, Supporting Information). The statistical results also demonstrate a better reproducibility of the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF enhancement via the hetero-SiC approach where the reproducibility of devices is critical in the practical manufacturing process. The good repeatability by hetero-SiC could be attributed to the seeding-guided growth rather than a random homogenous crystallization. The corresponding forward-reverse direction $J{-}V$ scans are shown in Figure 4B and exhibit a negligible hysteresis for both devices. The hetero-SiC approach also leads to stable steady-state power output (SPO) of $23.7\%$ which is higher than the SPO of the control device $(21.9\%)$ (Figure 4C). The corresponding external quantum efficiency (EQE) yields an integrated $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $23.5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for the control device and $23.8\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for the hetero-SiC device, which is in good agreement with the values from $J{-}V$ curves (within $5\%$ deviation). From the EQE measurement (Figure 4D) and UV absorption (Figure  4D inset), the hetero-SiC film exhibits the same bandgap as the control device $(1.54~\mathrm{eV})$ . Additionally, the dependence of $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ on the light intensity from 1 to $100\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ is examined. The $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ versus light intensity curves on a double-logarithmic scale (Figure  4E) can be fitted according to the relation of $J_{\mathrm{SC}}\propto\Phi^{\alpha};$ , where $\Phi$ is the light intensity and $\alpha$ is the exponent. The calculated $\alpha$ values are 0.99 for the control and 1.0 for the hetero-SiC PSCs, respectively. From the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ versus light intensity curves (Figure  4F), the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ is proportional to the logarithm of light intensity, and the slopes are calculated to be $1.89k_{\mathrm{B}}T/q$ for the control and $1.3k_{\mathrm{B}}T/q$ for the hetero-SiC PSCs, respectively, where $k_{\mathrm{B}}$ is the Boltzmann constant, $T$ is the absolute temperature in Kelvin, and $\boldsymbol{q}$ is the elementary charge. The enhanced slope of heteroSiC PSCs indicates that the hetero-SiC could significantly suppress the trap-assisted recombination and reduce the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ loss.
The electronic structure of the control film and hetero-SiC film is investigated via ultraviolet photoelectron emission spectra (UPS), shown in Figure  4G. The highest occupied valence band (VB) level, conduction band (CB) level, and work function (WF) are calculated and shown in Figure  4G inset. It is noted that the VB level for hetero-SiC perovskite film changed to $-5.6$ from $-5.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ (control film). The increased VB level of hetero-SiC reduces the energy barrier with SpiroOMeTAD $(5.22~\mathrm{eV})$ which enhances the hole collection rates and blocks undesirable electron transfer from the perovskite to the Spiro-OMeTAD. The photoluminescence (PL) spectroscopy is then conducted on the perovskite films to investigate the charge carrier dynamics (Figure 4H,I). The hetero-SiC film shows a threefold enhancement over the control film in the steady-state PL spectra of perovskite films, demonstrating that the hetero-SiC strategy has successfully suppressed recombination (Figure  4H). The time-resolved PL (TRPL) decay profiles (Figure  4I) were fitted via a bi-exponential decay model $(Y=A_{1}\mathrm{exp}(-t/\tau_{1})+A_{2}\mathrm{exp}(-t/\tau_{2}))$ , where $\tau_{1}$ and $\tau_{2}$ denote the fast and slow decay time constants, relating to the radiative and trap-assisted nonradiative recombination processes, respectively. Compared to the control film, the hetero-SiC film demonstrates a great enhancement in both $\tau_{1}$ (560.3  ns vs $78.8~\mathrm{ns})$ ) and $\tau_{2}$ (1710.2 ns vs 351.8 ns). In accordance with the $40\mathrm{\mV\}V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ enhancement, the TRPL results also show a significant suppression of trap-assisted nonradiative recombination and a lower defect density of perovskite layer. According to the previous findings, a number of factors can be attributed for the improvement in $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF of the hetero-SiC device. First, the hetero-SiC process facilitates the perovskite crystallization and leading to an enlarged grain size and high crystal quality. The larger grain size could greatly reduce the grain boundaries which benefits the suppression of charge carrier recombination, resulting in a better performance.[28] Second, the reduced energy barrier between perovskite layer and Spiro-OMeTAD facilitates the carrier collection rates. This is another important reason for the increase in $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF.[32] Lastly, the passivation function of ionic salts plays an important role on the performance enhancement, such as $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ (Table 2).[38]
Currently, the PSCs are on the eve of commercialization and the stability of PSCs is the most urgent issue to be solved. The stability measurements are then conducted on control and hetero-SiC devices (Figure  5). Ten devices for each group are prepared for the stability measurements. For storage stability, the nonencapsulated devices were stored in a dry box with $20\text{\textperthousand}$ relative humidity (RH). Compared to the control cells, which maintain ${\approx}82\%$ of their initial PCE over $2300\mathrm{~h~}$ storage, the hetero-SiC devices exhibit much better storage stability $91\%$ of their initial PCEs). Apart from the storage stability, the hetero-SiC devices also exhibit a great enhancement in both thermal stability and light stability. For thermal stability, the hetero-SiC devices maintain $77\%$ of their original PCE over $1400\mathrm{~h~}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ thermal annealing, while the control devices only keep $56\%$ of their initial performance. And for the light stability, the hetero-SiC devices demonstrate better stability $84\%$ of initial PCE) over $1400\mathrm{~h~}$ continuous light soaking. In comparison, the control device only maintains $30\%$ of initial PCE after $1400\mathrm{~h~}$ light soaking which indicates the hetero-SiC devices have much better performance on long time operation, which could be attributed to the reduced defects of perovskite film via hetero-SiC and passivation of ionic anion and cation.
From the morphology characterization results, the hetero-SiC film exhibits an enlarged grain size which could greatly reduce the grain-boundary density. The reduced grain-boundary density can effectively prevent water dispersion over crystal grain. From Figure S9 in the Supporting Information, two drops of water $2\ \upmu\mathrm{L},$ $1\mathrm{cm}$ distance) are dropped onto the control film and hetero-SiC film at the same distance. The water droplets on the control film spread quickly and are merged. Importantly, the hetero-SiC perovskite layer with a larger grain size can stop water droplets from spreading and hold their shape. Water droplets on the two films have comparable static contact angles. $(59.8^{\circ}$ for the control film and $60.2^{\circ}$ for the hetero-SiC film). This is because a small amount of $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ can just modify the crystallization process and has little influence on film hydrophobic property. Overall, the hetero-SiC technique not only boosts device performance but also significantly increases device stability. The enhanced stability of the hetero-SiC device could be attributed to the enhanced film quality, enlarged grain size, and reduced defect density.
## 3. Conclusion
The seeding-induced crystallization strategy for the one-step spin-coating perovskite fabrication method is revealed. The $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bu})_{2}\mathrm{PMeHBF}_{4}$ is added to the precursor which formed a low-solubility complex with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ . We take the advantage of the solubility difference between seeding material and perovskite material for the seed-induced crystallization. The seeds can effectively reduce the perovskite crystallization energy barrier and facilitate the growth of perovskite film. By employing multiple in situ analysis tools, the proposed hetero-SiC approach is proven to be an effective way to facilitate perovskite crystallization. The hetero-SiC films exhibit large grain sizes with reduced defects which results in PSCs PCEs up to $24.0\%$ and a $40~\mathrm{mV}$ $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ enhancement. Furthermore, the hetero-SiC PSCs outperform the control device in terms of stability, maintaining
$91\%$ of the initial PCE over $2300\mathrm{~h~}$ of storage stability, $77\%$ over $1400\mathrm{~h~}$ of $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ thermal annealing, and $84\%$ over $1400\mathrm{~h~}$ of light irradiation. Last but not least, the as proposed heteroSiC methodology offers a useful example of how to put the classical theory (seeding-induced crystallization) into reality while manipulating perovskite growth, a crucial area of investigation in the research of PSCs.
## 4. Experimental Section
Material: The $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ colloid precursor was purchased from Alfa Aesar. Formamidinium iodide (FAI), methylammonium bromide (MABr), and methylammonium chloride (MACl) were bought from Greatcell Energy. Di-tert-butyl(methyl)phosphonium tetrafluoroborate $((\mathsf{t}{\cdot}\mathsf{B u})_{2}\mathsf{P M e H B F}_{4})$ , cesium iodide (CsI), chlorobenzene (CB), acetonitrile (ACN), bis(trifluoromethane) sulfonimide lithium salt (Li-TFSI), 4-tertbutylpyridine (tBP), DMF, and DMSO were obtained from SigmaAldrich. Spiro-OMeTAD, lead iodide $(\mathsf{P b l}_{2})$ , and ITO substrates were purchased from Advanced Election Technology Co., Ltd. All chemicals were used without any further purification process.
Device Fabrication: The ITO glass was cleaned in detergent, deionized water, acetone, and isopropyl alcohol (IPA) sequentially. After being dried by $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ blowing, the cleaned ITO substrates were treated with ultraviolet ozone for $15\mathrm{\min}$ to remove organic residues and enhance surface wetting. Then the prepared $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ precursor (diluted by water with $\mathsf{S n O}_{2};\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}=\mathsf{I};4,\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v},$ ) was spin-coated onto the ITO substrates at 4000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ , followed by thermal annealing at ${150~^{\circ}C}$ for $30~\mathsf{m i n}$ in ambient air. The ${\mathsf{I T O}}/{\mathsf{S n O}}_{2}$ samples were treated with ultraviolet ozone for 10 min before the deposition of perovskite film. The perovskite precursor of $\(\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3})_{0.93}(\mathsf{M A P b B r}_{3})_{0.04}(\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3})_{0.03}$ was prepared by dissolving 1.4 m $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ , $0.06\textsf{M M A P b B r}_{3}$ , and $0.045\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{M}\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}$ and $0.5~\mathsf{m}$ MACl into 1  mL DMF/DMSO $({8}{:}]{\ v}/{\ v})$ solvent ( $10\%$ excess $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ was used to boost device performance) followed by $2h$ mechanical shaking for the solution to clear. The perovskite precursor was then spin-coated onto ${\mathsf{I T O}}/{\mathsf{S n O}}_{2}$ by two consecutive spin-coating steps ( $.7000\ r p m\ 5\ s$ and 5000  rpm 30  s). During the spin-coating process, the antisolvent (chlorobenzene) was poured onto the perovskite film at $75s$ . The intermediate phase film was annealed under ${\mathsf{120}}\ {\mathsf{^{o}C}}$ for $60\ \mathsf{m i n}$ in the glove box. After that, a thin layer of 2d perovskite was formed by coating BABr $\langle3\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ in IPA) onto perovskite film at $5000~\mathsf{r p m}$ , followed by a 10  min $100~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ annealing process. Then, the hole-transporting layer of $^{2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}}$ -tetrakis(N,N-di-p-methoxyphenylamine)- $^{.9,9^{\prime}}$ -spirobifluorene (Spiro-OMeTAD) was deposited onto the top of the perovskite layer at 3500  rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ with the composition of $72.8~\mathsf{m g}$ Spiro-OMeTAD, $78.8~\upmu\up L$ bis(trifluoromethane) sulfonimide lithium salt (Li-TFSI) stock solution ( $\cdot520~\mathsf{m g}$ Li-TFSI in $7m L$ acetonitrile), $28.8~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ tBP, and 1  mL chlorobenzene. Finally, $80~\mathsf{n m}$ of Au was thermally evaporated as a counter electrode using a shadow mask. For the hetero-SiC approach, different amounts of $(\mathrm{t}{\cdot}\mathsf{B u})_{2}\mathsf{P M e H B F}_{4}$ were directly dissolved into the perovskite precursor solution. The remaining steps were similar to the fabrication of control devices.
Characterizations and Measurements: The XRD patterns of perovskite films were collected from Rigaku SmartLab 9KW-Advanced X-ray diffractometer with $\mathsf{C u}\ \mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation in a step of $0.07^{\circ}$ and $\theta-2\theta$ scan mode. GIWAXS measurements were carried out with a Xeuss $2.0\mathsf{S A X S}/$ WAXS laboratory beamline using a Cu X-ray source $(8.05\mathrm{\keV},\ 1.54\mathrm{\AA})$ and a Pilatus3R 300K detector. The incidence angle is $0.3^{\circ}$ , except for the stated experiments. The FESEM images were collected from a fieldemission scanning electron microscope (Tescan VEGA3). The steadystate PL spectra and TRPL were measured on the Edinburgh FLSP920 spectrophotometer equipped with the excitation source of $485\:\:\mathsf{n m}$ picosecond pulsed diode laser at an average power of $0.15~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{W}.$ The absorption spectra of perovskite films were measured by the UV–vis spectrophotometer (CARY5000, Varian). The PL measurement was conducted on an Edinburgh FLSP920 spectrophotometer equipped with a $485\ n m$ picosecond pulsed diode. The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy and UPS were measured from the X-ray photoelectron spectrometer system (Thermo scientific Nexsa) equipped with a $12~\mathsf{k V}$ aluminum Ka X-ray.
The in situ UV–vis absorption was measured by an F20 spectrometer (Filmetrics, Inc.) with the halogen lamp $(1.5~\mathsf{m m})$ light source. A blank quartz substrate was used as the reference to calibrate the measurement signal. The absorbance of the thin film was calculated by the equation $A=-\mathsf{l o g}_{10}(T)$ , where $\tau$ is the transmission of the drying kinetics and crystallization rate of the perovskite thin film under ambient air conditions.[39] The in situ XRD and in situ SEM were also conducted from Rigaku SmartLab 9KW-Advanced X-ray diffractometer and FESEM (Tescan VEGA3), where the detail measurement method was adopted from ref. [36].
Compositional depth profiling of perovskite films was carried out using a ToF-SIMS 5 system from IONTOF, operated in the spectral mode and using a $25\mathrm{\keV\Bi^{3+}}$ primary ion beam with an ion current of $0.7~\mathsf{p A}.$ A mass-resolving power of about $8000\ m\ \Delta m^{-1}$ was reached. For depth profiling, a $3\ensuremath{\mathrm{\keV\Cs^{+}}}$ sputter beam with a current of $28{\mathsf{n A}}$ was used to remove material layer by layer in interlaced mode, from a raster area of $300~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\times300~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Mass spectrometry was performed on an area of $100\upmu\mathrm{m}\times100\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in the center of the sputter coater. A low-energy electronflood gun was used for charge compensation. In the positive polarity, the $[{\mathsf{S n O}}+{\mathsf{C s}}]^{+}$ secondary ion was the SnO fragment ionized through interaction with the sputter ion ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{s}}^{+}$ , yielding the highest signal-to-noise ratio positive-secondary-ion signal characteristic of the ITO substrate.
The current–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ characteristic and steady-state power output of photovoltaic devices were measured by Keithley 2400 Source Meter under standard AM $\mathsf{I}.5\mathsf{C}$ illumination using a solar simulator (Enli Tech, Taiwan), and the light intensity was calibrated by KG-5 silicon diode. The step voltage and scan speeds were $20~\mathsf{m V}$ and $\mathsf{l}00\ m\vee\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ . The EQE measurement was carried out with an EQE system (Enli Tech, Taiwan) using ${\mathsf{100}}\mathsf{H z}$ chopped monochromatic light ranging from 300 to $900\mathsf{n m}$ . Thermal-stability measurement was conducted by measuring the unencapsulated devices on an $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ hot plate in the glove box. Photostability was measured using encapsulated devices under a white light-emitting diode array with the intensity equal to 0.8 suns AM 1.5G, calibrated by a standard silicon photodiode detector (equipped with a KG-5 filter).
